    {
      "@id": "concept:australian-border-force-act-2015-sec-34",
      "@type": "cml:Concept",
      "skos:prefLabel": "34 Alcohol screening test—suspicion that Immigration and Border",
      "cml:realizedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/australian-border-force-act-2015#sec-34"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:schemaVersion": "2",
      "cml:proposedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml"
      },
      "prov:wasDerivedFrom": {
        "@id": "australian-border-force-act-2015.pdf"
      },
      "dc:source": "australian-border-force-act-2015.pdf, p.36",
      "values:originalText": "Protection worker under the influence of alcohol\n\n(1) An authorised person may require an Immigration and Border\nProtection worker to undergo an alcohol screening test if:\n\n(a) the worker is in the course of performing his or her duties as\nan Immigration and Border Protection worker; and\n\n(b) the authorised person reasonably suspects that the worker is\nunder the influence of alcohol.\nCompliance with requirement\n\n(2) The worker must comply with a requirement given to him or her\nunder subsection (1).\nNote 1:\nSee subsection 13(4) and sections 15, 28 and 29 of the Public Service\nAct 1999 for the consequences of an APS employee in the Department\nnot complying with subsection (2) of this section.\nNote 2:\nSee section 57 of this Act for the termination of a person’s\nengagement as a consultant or contractor, or for the arranging of a\nperson to cease to perform services for the Department, if the person\ndoes not comply with subsection (2) of this section.\nReturn to duties\n\n(3) If the result of the alcohol screening test shows that alcohol is not\npresent in the worker’s breath, he or she may return to his or her\nduties immediately.\n\n\nAlcohol and drug tests  Part 5\n\nSection 35\n\nAustralian Border Force Act 2015\n\nCompilation date: 04/06/2026\n\nRelationship with section 35\n\n(4) This section does not limit section 35.",
      "skos:definition": "Protection worker under the influence of alcohol\n\n(1) An authorised person may require an Immigration and Border\nProtection worker to undergo an alcohol screening test if:\n\n(a) the worker is in the course of performing his or her duties as\nan Immigration and Border Protection worker; and\n\n(b) the authorised person reasonably suspects that the worker is\nunder the influence of alcohol.\nCompliance with requirement\n\n(2) The worker must comply with a requirement given to him or her\nunder subsection (1).\nNote 1:\nSee subsection 13(4) and sections 15, 28 and 29 of the Public Service\nAct 1999 for the consequences of an APS employee in the Department\nnot complying with subsection (2) of this section.\nNote 2:\nSee section 57 of this Act for the termination of a person’s\nengagement as a consultant or contractor, or for the arranging of a\nperson to cease to perform services for the Department, if the person\ndoes not comply with subsection (2) of this section.\nReturn to duties\n\n(3) If the result of the alcohol screening test shows that alcohol is not\npresent in the worker’s breath, he or she may return to his or her\nduties immediately.\n\n\nAlcohol and drug tests  Part 5\n\nSection 35\n\nAustralian Border Force Act 2015\n\nCompilation date: 04/06/2026\n\nRelationship with section 35\n\n(4) This section does not limit section 35.",
      "cml:hasPart": [
        {
          "@id": "concept:australian-border-force-act-2015-sec-34-ss-1"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:australian-border-force-act-2015-sec-34-ss-2"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:australian-border-force-act-2015-sec-34-ss-3"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:australian-border-force-act-2015-sec-34-ss-4"
        }
      ],
      "cml:integrityHash": "d455453e9b0f3d4fb03f56c96c0834a26a1a5405eb1c1338e187a817e1755abf"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/australian-border-force-act-2015#sec-34",
      "@type": "cof:Section",
      "cof:title": "34 Alcohol screening test—suspicion that Immigration and Border",
      "values:kind": "section",
      "cof:pageNumber": 36,
      "values:originalText": "Protection worker under the influence of alcohol\n\n(1) An authorised person may require an Immigration and Border\nProtection worker to undergo an alcohol screening test if:\n\n(a) the worker is in the course of performing his or her duties as\nan Immigration and Border Protection worker; and\n\n(b) the authorised person reasonably suspects that the worker is\nunder the influence of alcohol.\nCompliance with requirement\n\n(2) The worker must comply with a requirement given to him or her\nunder subsection (1).\nNote 1:\nSee subsection 13(4) and sections 15, 28 and 29 of the Public Service\nAct 1999 for the consequences of an APS employee in the Department\nnot complying with subsection (2) of this section.\nNote 2:\nSee section 57 of this Act for the termination of a person’s\nengagement as a consultant or contractor, or for the arranging of a\nperson to cease to perform services for the Department, if the person\ndoes not comply with subsection (2) of this section.\nReturn to duties\n\n(3) If the result of the alcohol screening test shows that alcohol is not\npresent in the worker’s breath, he or she may return to his or her\nduties immediately.\n\n\nAlcohol and drug tests  Part 5\n\nSection 35\n\nAustralian Border Force Act 2015\n\nCompilation date: 04/06/2026\n\nRelationship with section 35\n\n(4) This section does not limit section 35."
    },
