    {
      "@id": "concept:australian-border-force-act-2015-sec-54",
      "@type": "cml:Concept",
      "skos:prefLabel": "54 Delegation by Comptroller-General of Customs",
      "cml:realizedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/australian-border-force-act-2015#sec-54"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:schemaVersion": "2",
      "cml:proposedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml"
      },
      "prov:wasDerivedFrom": {
        "@id": "australian-border-force-act-2015.pdf"
      },
      "dc:source": "australian-border-force-act-2015.pdf, p.55",
      "values:originalText": "(1) The Comptroller-General of Customs may, by writing, delegate\nany of his or her functions or powers under a law of the\nCommonwealth (other than section 179, 179K or 179L of the\nCustoms Act 1901) to:\n\n(a) the Secretary; or\n\n(b) an Immigration and Border Protection worker.\nDirections to delegates\n\n(2) An Immigration and Border Protection worker must, in performing\nfunctions or exercising powers under a delegation under\nsubsection (1), comply with any written directions of the\nComptroller-General of Customs.\nSubdelegation by Secretary\n\n(3) If the Secretary is delegated functions or powers under\nsubsection (1), the Secretary may, by writing, delegate any of those\nfunctions or powers to an Immigration and Border Protection\nworker.\n\n(4) An Immigration and Border Protection worker must, in performing\nfunctions or exercising powers under a delegation under\nsubsection (3), comply with any written directions of the Secretary.\n\n(5) The Secretary must not give directions under subsection (4) in\nrelation to particular functions or powers that are inconsistent with\nany directions given under subsection (2) in relation to those\nfunctions or powers.\n\n(6) A function that is performed or a power that is exercised by an\nImmigration and Border Protection worker under a delegation\nunder subsection (3) is taken, for the purposes of the law referred\nto in subsection (1), to have been performed or exercised by the\nComptroller-General of Customs.",
      "skos:definition": "(1) The Comptroller-General of Customs may, by writing, delegate\nany of his or her functions or powers under a law of the\nCommonwealth (other than section 179, 179K or 179L of the\nCustoms Act 1901) to:\n\n(a) the Secretary; or\n\n(b) an Immigration and Border Protection worker.\nDirections to delegates\n\n(2) An Immigration and Border Protection worker must, in performing\nfunctions or exercising powers under a delegation under\nsubsection (1), comply with any written directions of the\nComptroller-General of Customs.\nSubdelegation by Secretary\n\n(3) If the Secretary is delegated functions or powers under\nsubsection (1), the Secretary may, by writing, delegate any of those\nfunctions or powers to an Immigration and Border Protection\nworker.\n\n(4) An Immigration and Border Protection worker must, in performing\nfunctions or exercising powers under a delegation under\nsubsection (3), comply with any written directions of the Secretary.\n\n(5) The Secretary must not give directions under subsection (4) in\nrelation to particular functions or powers that are inconsistent with\nany directions given under subsection (2) in relation to those\nfunctions or powers.\n\n(6) A function that is performed or a power that is exercised by an\nImmigration and Border Protection worker under a delegation\nunder subsection (3) is taken, for the purposes of the law referred\nto in subsection (1), to have been performed or exercised by the\nComptroller-General of Customs.",
      "cml:hasPart": [
        {
          "@id": "concept:australian-border-force-act-2015-sec-54-ss-1"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:australian-border-force-act-2015-sec-54-ss-2"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:australian-border-force-act-2015-sec-54-ss-3"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:australian-border-force-act-2015-sec-54-ss-4"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:australian-border-force-act-2015-sec-54-ss-5"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:australian-border-force-act-2015-sec-54-ss-6"
        }
      ],
      "cml:integrityHash": "3e9fa021a4d9de249fb09690f0dd18d889b7d6f0fe8c7e48ade8324b93e45823"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/australian-border-force-act-2015#sec-54",
      "@type": "cof:Section",
      "cof:title": "54 Delegation by Comptroller-General of Customs",
      "values:kind": "section",
      "cof:pageNumber": 55,
      "values:originalText": "(1) The Comptroller-General of Customs may, by writing, delegate\nany of his or her functions or powers under a law of the\nCommonwealth (other than section 179, 179K or 179L of the\nCustoms Act 1901) to:\n\n(a) the Secretary; or\n\n(b) an Immigration and Border Protection worker.\nDirections to delegates\n\n(2) An Immigration and Border Protection worker must, in performing\nfunctions or exercising powers under a delegation under\nsubsection (1), comply with any written directions of the\nComptroller-General of Customs.\nSubdelegation by Secretary\n\n(3) If the Secretary is delegated functions or powers under\nsubsection (1), the Secretary may, by writing, delegate any of those\nfunctions or powers to an Immigration and Border Protection\nworker.\n\n(4) An Immigration and Border Protection worker must, in performing\nfunctions or exercising powers under a delegation under\nsubsection (3), comply with any written directions of the Secretary.\n\n(5) The Secretary must not give directions under subsection (4) in\nrelation to particular functions or powers that are inconsistent with\nany directions given under subsection (2) in relation to those\nfunctions or powers.\n\n(6) A function that is performed or a power that is exercised by an\nImmigration and Border Protection worker under a delegation\nunder subsection (3) is taken, for the purposes of the law referred\nto in subsection (1), to have been performed or exercised by the\nComptroller-General of Customs."
    },
